---
title: Product page
layout: home
description: One thing you own — how long you have had it, what it replaced, what is wrong with it, and whether you would buy it again. The review a specification sheet cannot be.
eyebrow: Collections · Uses
source: src/components/ad.css · content.css
---
<section class="im-section im-wrap" style="padding-top: 1.5rem; --im-section-gap: 1.5rem">
	<p class="im-eyebrow">Collections · Uses · {{page.title}}</p>
	<p class="im-lede">{{page.description}}</p>
	<p class="doc-source"><code>{{page.source}}</code></p>
</section>

<div class="im-single im-wrap" data-sidebar="on">
	<article class="im-stack" style="--im-stack-gap: 1.5rem">
		{{#with demo.uses.[0]}}
		<nav aria-label="Breadcrumb">
			<ol class="im-breadcrumb im-breadcrumb-chevron">
				<li><a href="/">{{> "icons/house"}}<span>Home</span></a></li>
				<li><a href="/collections/uses/">{{> "icons/package"}}<span>Uses</span></a></li>
				<li><span aria-current="page">{{name}}</span></li>
			</ol>
		</nav>

		<figure class="im-shot"><img src="{{../demo.shots.[0]}}" alt="" loading="eager"><figcaption><b>Two years in</b> Scratched, and none of the scratches are on the glass.</figcaption></figure>

		<header class="im-stack" style="--im-stack-gap: .5rem">
			<p><span class="im-kind">{{icon "package"}}Uses</span></p>
			<h1 class="im-collhead-title" style="font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 1.3rem + 2vw, 2.5rem)">{{name}}</h1>
			<p class="im-exp-where"><span>{{by}}</span><span>Owned since March 2024</span></p>
		</header>

		<div class="im-expstats">
			<span><strong>{{price}}</strong>Paid</span>
			<span><strong>2 yrs</strong>Owned</span>
			<span><strong>Daily</strong>Used</span>
			<span><strong>Yes</strong>Buy again</span>
		</div>
		{{/with}}

		<section class="im-content im-content-flush im-content-lede" data-im-zoom>
			<p>Every picture on this site since March was taken on it, which is the only recommendation that means anything.</p>

			<h2>What it replaced</h2>
			<p>A full-frame body and two lenses that lived in a cupboard because carrying them was a decision. This one lives in a coat pocket and therefore gets used, and a camera that gets used beats a better camera that does not.</p>

			<h2>What is wrong with it</h2>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>The battery.</strong> Two hundred frames, optimistically. Buy three spares and stop thinking about it.</li>
				<li><strong>The fixed lens.</strong> This is the point, and it is still sometimes the problem.</li>
				<li><strong>The price.</strong> It is a lot of money for a camera that does one thing.</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Would I buy it again</h2>
			<p>Yes, at this price, knowing about the battery. Not at more.</p>
		</section>
		{{#with demo.uses.[0]}}
		<p class="im-cluster"><a class="im-btn im-btn-primary" href="#" rel="sponsored noopener">{{icon "external-link"}}Where I got it</a><a class="im-btn" href="/collections/uses/">Everything else I use</a></p>
		<p class="im-affiliate-note" style="border-top: 1px solid var(--im-line); padding-top: .75rem">Affiliate link — I earn a little if you buy, at no cost to you</p>
		{{/with}}
	</article>

	<aside>
		<div class="im-sticky-rail">
			<div class="im-widgets">
				<section class="im-widget" aria-labelledby="u-facts">
					<h2 class="im-widget-title" id="u-facts">The facts</h2>
					<dl class="im-widget-facts">
						<dt>Paid</dt><dd>€1,599</dd>
						<dt>Bought</dt><dd>Mar 2024</dd>
						<dt>Replaced</dt><dd>A whole bag</dd>
						<dt>Checked</dt><dd>Sep 2026</dd>
					</dl>
				</section>

				<section class="im-widget" aria-labelledby="u-more">
					<h2 class="im-widget-title" id="u-more">Also on the desk <a href="/collections/uses/">All</a></h2>
					<ul class="im-widget-list im-widget-list-thumbs">
						{{#each demo.uses}}{{#match @index ">" 0}}<li><a href="/collections/uses/page/"><img src="{{lookup ../demo.shots @index}}" alt="" loading="lazy">{{name}}<small>{{price}}</small></a></li>{{/match}}{{/each}}
					</ul>
				</section>
			</div>
		</div>
	</aside>
</div>

<section class="im-section im-wrap">
	<div class="im-prose">
		<h2>Four headings, and the third is the one that matters</h2>
		<p><strong>What it replaced</strong>, <strong>what is wrong with it</strong>, <strong>would I buy it again</strong>. A page that only praises is an advertisement, and readers can tell — the three specific complaints are what make the recommendation worth anything.</p>
		<p>The stats row carries what a specification sheet would: paid, owned, how often, buy-again. Four facts, and the last one is the review.</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>One photograph, of yours.</strong> Scratched, dusty, on your desk — not the press shot. The caption should say how old it is.</li>
			<li><strong>The disclosure sits at the bottom too</strong>, next to the link it is about.</li>
			<li><strong>Say when the page was last checked.</strong> It is in the rail, and it is the difference between a recommendation and an artefact.</li>
		</ul>
	</div>
</section>
